//@version=6

// Mansfield Relative Strength, for my weekly stage-analysis screener.
// I calculate this the way Weinstein/Mansfield define it: take the
// stock's price relative to a market index (RS_ratio), then compare
// today's ratio against its own moving average and express the gap
// as a percentage. Above zero means the stock is outperforming the
// index over the lookback window; below zero means it's lagging.
// Default lookback is 52, matching a weekly chart's ~1-year window.
indicator(title="Mansfield Relative Strength", shorttitle="MRS", overlay=false)

periodInput = input.int(52, title="SMA Period", minval=2)
indexSymbolInput = input.symbol("SPX", title="Comparison Index")

// I pull the index's close on the same timeframe as the chart this is
// plotted on, so it works whether I'm looking at daily or weekly bars.
indexClose = request.security(indexSymbolInput, timeframe.period, close)

// RS_ratio is the raw Weinstein relative-strength figure: stock price
// divided by index price. MRS then normalizes it against its own SMA
// so the line oscillates around zero instead of drifting with price.
rsRatio = close / indexClose
smaRS = ta.sma(rsRatio, periodInput)

// I don't have a full SMA window yet on the first (periodInput - 1)
// bars, so I plot na there instead of an incorrect partial value.
mrs = bar_index >= periodInput - 1 ? 100 * ((rsRatio / smaRS) - 1) : na

plot(mrs, title="Mansfield RS", color=color.blue, style=plot.style_line, linewidth=2)

// Single zero reference line only. Above zero is long-term positive,
// below zero is long-term negative, per Mansfield's own reading of it.
hline(0, title="Zero Line", color=color.gray, linestyle=hline.style_dashed)
